{
 "format": 1,
 "about": "Site sign-in rules the host runs as data (Gurum SignInAgent + SigninRules). Each rule: the sites it covers, a one-line note for the sign-in head, the ways to offer (only these), each way's session page ({return} = the address to come back to once signed in) and the words of the control that starts it on that page. A way with requires_app is offered only when that app is on the phone. pc_view_sites: [{hosts, width, os}] -- sites opened in a full PC viewport on phones (laid out at width CSS px, default 1366, and scaled down into the phone browser pane with touch coordinate mapping; screenshots are captured at width px; os is \"windows\" or \"mac\" for desktop Chrome UA, Sec-CH-UA-Platform/Mobile ?0 client hints and document-start navigator.platform/maxTouchPoints=0/screen.width-height/devicePixelRatio=1; restored when leaving the site). Coexists with desktop_sites so older apps that do not read pc_view_sites fall back to desktop_sites. desktop_sites: sites opened as the desktop site (what Chrome's \"Desktop site\" menu does) because their mobile web stops the sign-in with an app-only notice. provider_password: companies whose sign-in screen keeps its own password -- the host never asks for it, keeps it or types it there; the user types it on that company's own screen (never = on every screen of that company; for_other_sites = only on its screen reached through a \"Sign in with <company>\" way on another site). site_notes: a one-line note for the sign-in head on a site that needs no rule of its own.",
 "pc_view_sites": [
  {
   "hosts": [
    "gov.kr",
    "www.gov.kr",
    "plus.gov.kr",
    "anyid.go.kr",
    "console.cloud.google.com"
   ],
   "width": 1366,
   "os": "windows"
  }
 ],
 "desktop_sites": [
  "gov.kr"
 ],
 "chrome_sites": [
  "*"
 ],
 "webview_sites": [
  "naver.com"
 ],
 "provider_password": {
  "never": [],
  "for_other_sites": [
   "apple.com",
   "facebook.com",
   "live.com",
   "microsoftonline.com"
  ]
 },
 "rules": [
  {
   "id": "google",
   "sites": [
    "google.com"
   ],
   "note": "Google (accounts.google.com -- Google's own sign-in and \"Sign in with Google\" on other sites): ALWAYS use the user's Google e-mail (\"login\") and Google password (\"password\") -- NEVER choose passkey. If Google shows a passkey prompt/screen first, press \"Try another way\" / \"다른 방법 시도\" and choose \"Enter your password\" / \"비밀번호 입력\". When Google asks to choose a 2-step verification method, list EVERY method with its exact full text as shown on screen (e.g. \"휴대전화나 태블릿에서 예를 선택합니다\", \"스마트폰 또는 태블릿을 사용하여 보안 코드 받기\", \"•••43번으로 인증 코드를 받습니다\") -- NEVER collapse distinct methods to \"일회용 번호\". When Google shows the phone prompt verification screen (\"휴대전화나 태블릿을 확인하세요\" / \"Check your phone\" with a large 2-digit number such as 15), ALWAYS return {\"action\": \"ask\", \"ask\": \"phone\", \"show\": \"<exact number e.g. 15>\"} so the user immediately sees the number to tap on their phone -- NEVER return \"code\" or \"person\". When the account chooser lists the user's account, press it. Once signed in, go back to the original sign-in."
  },
  {
   "id": "naver",
   "sites": [
    "naver.com"
   ],
   "note": "NAVER (naver.com and every NAVER service, and \"Sign in with NAVER\" on other sites): in this in-app browser the ID and password and the passkey do not work -- never type them and never list them as ways. Sign in with the NAVER app when it is on this phone (its \"앱 열기\" / \"Open app\" control) -- the QR code is for scanning with another device: NAVER refuses a QR code approved in the NAVER app on this same phone (\"QR로그인 실패\", measured 2026-11-15). Once signed in, go back to the original sign-in.",
   "return_param": "url",
   "ways": [
    {
     "id": "qr",
     "kind": "qr",
     "name": {
      "ko": "QR 코드(다른 기기로 찍기)",
      "en": "a QR code (scan it with another device)"
     },
     "page": "https://nid.naver.com/nidlogin.login?mode=form&url={return}",
     "press": [
      "QR 코드 로그인",
      "QR sign-in"
     ]
    },
    {
     "id": "app",
     "kind": "app",
     "name": {
      "ko": "네이버 앱",
      "en": "the NAVER app"
     },
     "page": "https://nid.naver.com/nidlogin.login?mode=form&svctype=262144&url={return}",
     "press": [
      "앱 열기",
      "Open app"
     ],
     "requires_app": "com.nhn.android.search"
    }
   ]
  }
 ],
 "site_notes": [
  {
   "sites": [
    "gov.kr",
    "plus.gov.kr"
   ],
   "note": "Government24 (gov.kr / plus.gov.kr): (1) The sign-in page shows auth category tabs/cards (\"간편인증\", \"모바일 신분증\", \"금융인증서\", \"공동인증서\", \"아이디 로그인\"). Match the user's chosen method: if the user chose a specific simple-auth app (e.g. 카카오톡, 네이버, 통신사PASS, 토스, KB국민인증서, 신한인증서, 삼성패스), press \"간편인증\" first to open the simple-auth modal. (2) Inside the 간편인증 modal, FIRST click the chosen private certificate provider icon in the left list and confirm on the next screen that that provider is visibly selected/highlighted (\"선택됨\"). NEVER fill or submit while a different provider is highlighted. (3) Then fill the right-side personal verification form using the user's own details (account: false — name, birthdate as 8 digits YYYYMMDD, phone split into 010 select + remaining 8 digits), tick \"전체동의\" (required terms), and press \"인증 요청\". (4) Once the request is sent, ask \"phone\" so the user approves in that app on their phone; after they return, press \"인증 완료\". (5) NEVER mix \"간편인증\" personal details (name/birthdate/phone) with \"아이디 로그인\" (login/password): only type login/password when the user explicitly chose \"아이디 로그인\"."
  },
  {
   "sites": [
    "weee.com"
   ],
   "note": "Weee! (weee.com) has two sign-in entries. (1) The first-visit pop-up (\"SIGN UP NOW TO CLAIM OFFER\") IS a sign-in layer with \"Continue with Google\", \"Continue with Facebook\" and \"Continue with email\" -- not an ad to close when the chosen way is on it; its X (top right) only closes it. (2) The sign-in page /en/account/login (\"Let's get started\"): an e-mail field and \"Continue\", then \"Google\", \"Facebook\" and \"Phone\" under \"Or continue with\". Each Google button has Google's own button laid over it -- on the pop-up a nearly invisible Google sign-in frame (\"Google 계정으로 로그인 버튼\") over \"Continue with Google\", on the page a transparent \"Google OAuth Login\" control over the \"Google\" chip: that cover IS the same button. Press \"Continue with Google\" / \"Google\" once; it opens Google's sign-in (a new window from the pop-up, the same tab from the page). No other window covers these buttons -- never look for one to close. The e-mail way: e-mail, then \"Continue\"; for an e-mail with no account the next screen is \"Verify account\" asking a 4-digit code sent by e-mail (what comes next for an existing account was not checked)."
  }
 ]
}
